<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} crumbs={[{href:'/guides/',label:'Guides'}]} current="Chinese calendar" article={{title,description,datePublished:'2026-11-06',dateModified:'2026-11-06'}} cta={false}>
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} /><div class="calendar-page"><ChinaCalendar /><article class="growth-article calendar-editorial">





<div class="calendar-trust"><span>2026–2027 coverage</span><span>China Standard Time · UTC+8</span><a href="#sources">Sources &amp; verification ↗</a><span>Updated 6 November 2026</span></div><section class="calendar-year-index" id="annual-calendar"><div><span class="cal-kicker">THE FULL YEAR, AT A GLANCE</span><h2>Chinese lunar calendar by year</h2><p>Browse every Gregorian and lunar date, or use the interactive month view above to check a specific day.</p></div>{[2026,2027].map(y=><details class="year-disclosure"><summary><span>{y} Chinese calendar</span><span>12 months · {y===2026?'Year of the Horse':'Year of the Goat'} ↗</span></summary><p>Zodiac years begin at Chinese New Year, not 1 January. January and early February may belong to the preceding zodiac year.</p><div class="annual-months">{Array.from({length:12},(_,m)=><details><summary>{new Date(Date.UTC(y,m,1)).toLocaleString('en',{month:'long',timeZone:'UTC'})}</summary><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>{y} Gregorian–lunar dates · {new Date(Date.UTC(y,m,1)).toLocaleString('en',{month:'long',timeZone:'UTC'})}</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Lunar date</th><th scope="col">Festival / holiday</th></tr></thead><tbody>{calendarDates.filter(d=>d.year===y&&d.month===m+1).map(d=><tr><th scope="row">{d.date}</th><td>{d.lunar}</td><td>{d.festival||d.holiday||'—'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details>)}</div></details>)}</section><section id="dates"><h2>Chinese calendar dates for 2026 and 2027</h2><p>These are the celebration dates, not the full holiday breaks. The lunar-date references follow the Hong Kong Observatory’s annual conversion tables; official mainland leave periods are listed separately below.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Major festivals and calendar dates</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Occasion</th><th scope="col">Calendar basis</th><th scope="col">2026</th><th scope="col">2027</th></tr></thead><tbody>{festivals.map(f=><tr><th scope="row">{f[0]}</th><td>{f[1]}</td><td>{f[2]}</td><td>{f[3]}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Lantern Festival, Qixi and Double Ninth are cultural observances; their presence in this table does not mean a nationwide day off. Hong Kong, Macao and Taiwan have their own holiday arrangements, so use the relevant jurisdiction’s schedule if your itinerary crosses borders.</p></section>
<section id="holidays"><h2>China public holiday calendar 2027</h2><p>The 2027 mainland holiday schedule was announced on 5 November 2026. The periods below are the announced breaks, including adjacent days incorporated into each break. They are not a statement that every employee receives that number of statutory paid holiday days.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Mainland China announced holiday breaks, 2027</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Holiday</th><th scope="col">Break</th><th scope="col">Planning implication</th></tr></thead><tbody>{holidays.map(h=><tr><th scope="row">{h[0]}</th><td>{h[1]} 2027</td><td>Confirm appointments and transport before booking.</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Source: <a href="https://www.xinhuanet.com/20261105/d91d22dfe8aa4cd7a3bdbc0ad67ef68b/c.html">Xinhua’s summary of the 2027 State Council holiday announcement</a>. For the complete arrangement, including designated weekend workdays, consult the <a href="https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/content/202611/content_7085101.htm">State Council notice</a>.</p><h3>Why is a weekend sometimes a working day in China?</h3><p>Holiday arrangements may move work onto selected Saturdays or Sundays to create a longer continuous break. A normal weekday may therefore fall inside a holiday, and a weekend may be an official adjusted workday. Confirm the exact date rather than assuming Monday–Friday availability.</p><p>An adjusted workday does not guarantee that your supplier’s engineering team, production line, interpreter or driver is available. Conversely, some factories operate during part of a holiday. A written appointment is still necessary.</p><h3>Golden Week and travel planning</h3><p>For an October trip, check the National Day break before fixing your route. Keep business appointments separate from sightseeing plans: a destination may welcome visitors while an industrial site’s office is closed. Check hotel and train availability for the actual itinerary, and avoid scheduling a critical factory meeting immediately after an unconfirmed travel connection.</p></section>
<section id="factory"><h2>When do Chinese factories close for Chinese New Year?</h2><p>There is no single closure calendar covering every factory. The public holiday is a reference point. A supplier can stop production earlier, reopen its office before its production lines, or restart different workshops on different dates.</p><p>For example, <a href="https://www.jupiterresearch.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/2.0_Jupiter_CCELL_CNY2026_ImportantDates.pdf">CCELL’s published 2026 factory notice</a> gave its own closure period of 9–24 February. That is one company’s schedule, not a rule for China or a prediction for 2027.</p><div class="calendar-three-dates"><article><span>01</span><h3>Festival date</h3><p>A date in the cultural calendar. It does not define an entire break.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Official holiday</h3><p>The national leave period, with separately designated adjusted workdays.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Your factory’s schedule</h3><p>Actual production, staffing and appointment availability. Confirm with your supplier.</p></article></div><h3>“We are open” is not enough to plan a production visit</h3><p>Ask what will actually be operating. The sales team might be answering messages while the line you want to see is idle. A showroom meeting can still be useful, but it answers different questions from observing your product being manufactured.</p><ul><li>What is the last production day before the break?</li><li>When does the office reopen, and when does the relevant workshop restart?</li><li>Will the product or process we want to see be running on our proposed date?</li><li>Will the engineer, quality lead and commercial contact attend?</li><li>Are samples, tooling and agreed documents ready?</li><li>What is the fallback date if production or staffing changes?</li></ul><p>If you have an order in progress, confirm dispatch, inspection and carrier cutoffs separately. A factory reopening date is not a guaranteed shipping date. Keep formal audits and shipment inspections distinct from a <a href="/guides/factory-visit-vs-audit-vs-inspection/">hosted factory visit</a>.</p></section>
<section id="visit"><h2>Best time to visit factories in China: start with your purpose</h2><p>The best date is the one on which the relevant people, process and evidence are available. A pleasant travel month cannot compensate for an idle production line or an absent decision-maker. Use the holiday calendar to identify questions, then confirm the appointment with each supplier.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Match the timing to the work you need to do</caption><thead><tr><th>Visit purpose</th><th>What must be available</th><th>What to confirm</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>First supplier meeting</th><td>Commercial and technical contacts, relevant samples</td><td>Production address, meeting agenda and who can answer product questions</td></tr><tr><th>Observe manufacturing</th><td>The relevant line and production stage</td><td>Actual run schedule, outsourced steps and access limitations</td></tr><tr><th>Review samples or tooling</th><td>The correct sample version and responsible engineer</td><td>Readiness date, specifications and open decisions</td></tr><tr><th>Visit an existing supplier</th><td>People responsible for the outstanding issue</td><td>Evidence to review and a written follow-up owner</td></tr><tr><th>Film a factory</th><td>Written filming permission and an agreed process to film</td><td>Shot list, restricted areas, staffing and production availability</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Combine trade fairs with supplier visits</h3><p>A trade fair can help build a shortlist, but an exhibitor’s booth location is not necessarily its production location. Confirm the factory address before adding a visit to your route. For Guangzhou, use our <a href="/guides/canton-fair-to-factory-visits/">Canton Fair to factory visits guide</a> and <a href="/guides/visit-factories-between-canton-fair-phases/">guide to visiting between fair phases</a>. Check the current fair dates and product phases in the <a href="/china-trade-fairs/">trade fair directory</a>.</p><h3>Choose a region before adding more appointments</h3><p>Group confirmed addresses into a realistic route. An appointment in <a href="/destinations/shenzhen/">Shenzhen</a> and another in <a href="/destinations/yiwu/">Yiwu</a> require intercity planning; they are not interchangeable “China factory” stops. The <a href="/tools/china-manufacturing-hub-finder/">manufacturing hub finder</a> can help identify research starting points for your product.</p></section>

<section class="travel-checklist" id="travel-checklist"><div class="checklist-heading"><div><span class="cal-kicker">YOUR PRE-TRIP CHECK</span><h2>Ready when you are.</h2><p>Work through these six checks for each supplier.</p></div><span id="check-count" aria-live="polite">0 of 6 confirmed</span></div><progress id="check-progress" value="0" max="6" aria-label="Visit preparation progress"></progress><div class="check-items"><label><input type="checkbox"/><span>Confirm full dates and China time for every appointment.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"/><span>Check public holidays and adjusted workdays.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"/><span>Confirm the address, attending staff and production schedule.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"/><span>Allow time for travel, access formalities and notes.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"/><span>Arrange interpreting, technical access and filming permission.</span></label><label><input type="checkbox"/><span>Agree a fallback date if the supplier changes its schedule.</span></label></div><div class="checklist-actions"><a class="calendar-primary" href="/plan-my-trip/">Plan my factory visit ↗</a><a href="/services/factory-visit-planning/">What visit planning includes</a><button id="reset-checklist" type="button">Reset checklist</button></div><p class="checklist-footnote">Your checklist is saved only in this browser. It does not confirm a supplier appointment.</p></section>
<div class="calendar-background"><details><summary>How the Chinese calendar works</summary><section id="calendar"><h2>What is the Chinese calendar?</h2><p>The traditional Chinese calendar is lunisolar: its months follow the Moon, while its year stays aligned with the seasons through an intercalary, or leap, month when needed. “Chinese lunar calendar” is the familiar search term, but “lunisolar” describes the system more precisely.</p><p>A lunar month contains 29 or 30 days. Twelve lunar months are shorter than a solar year, so an extra month is inserted in certain years. That is why Chinese New Year and Mid-Autumn Festival move on a Western calendar. A leap lunar month is different from 29 February in a Gregorian leap year.</p><p>For a flight, hotel booking, purchase order or supplier appointment, use the Gregorian date. Write the month in words when confirming an international meeting: “6 February 2027” is clearer than “02/06/27”. Include China Standard Time, UTC+8, for calls and appointments.</p><h3>Lunar months, solar terms and festivals</h3><p>Lunar months are numbered within the traditional year. Solar terms divide the seasonal cycle into 24 parts; they are not all public holidays. Qingming is associated with a solar term, while Dragon Boat Festival follows a lunar month and day. Do not infer a day off simply because an almanac marks a festival or seasonal event.</p></section></details><details><summary>Chinese zodiac years explained</summary><section id="year"><h2>What year is it in the Chinese calendar?</h2><p>China uses the Gregorian year for ordinary civil and business purposes. Traditional years also have a zodiac animal and a name in the 60-year stem-and-branch cycle. There is no need to convert a supplier contract into a different numbered “Chinese year”.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Zodiac years using the Chinese New Year boundary</caption><thead><tr><th>Gregorian period</th><th>Traditional year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 January–16 February 2026</td><td>Year of the Snake, continuing from 2025</td></tr><tr><td>17 February 2026–5 February 2027</td><td>Year of the Horse · Bing-wu</td></tr><tr><td>From 6 February 2027</td><td>Year of the Goat · Ding-wei</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The zodiac does not switch on 1 January in this convention. Some astrology traditions use the Start of Spring solar term instead; this guide uses Chinese New Year for its year labels. Neither convention determines factory opening hours or commercial deadlines.</p></section></details></div><section id="faq"><h2>Chinese calendar questions</h2><details><summary>Is the Chinese calendar different from the calendar used for business?</summary><p>The traditional calendar is used for festivals and cultural dates. Standard business correspondence and bookings use Gregorian dates. Ask for a Gregorian date whenever a supplier describes a deadline using a lunar festival.</p></details><details><summary>How do I find today’s Chinese lunar date?</summary><p>First check the current date in China, which uses UTC+8. Then find that Gregorian date in the linked annual conversion table. Around midnight, your local date can differ from the date in China. The interactive calendar above highlights today in China and lets you select dates in 2026 and 2027.</p></details><details><summary>Is Chinese New Year always in February?</summary><p>No. Its Gregorian date changes between January and February. In the two years covered here, it falls on 17 February 2026 and 6 February 2027.</p></details><details><summary>Are all Chinese festivals public holidays?</summary><p>No. A cultural observance or solar term does not automatically create a nationwide break. Check the official leave arrangement separately from the festival calendar.</p></details><details><summary>Can I visit a factory during Chinese New Year?</summary><p>Only if that specific supplier has confirmed access, the people you need and the activity you want to observe. Do not assume an office appointment means production is running. If the goal is to assess a manufacturing process, agree a date when that process will be visible.</p></details><details><summary>Do Chinese factories work on Saturdays and Sundays?</summary><p>Schedules vary. Some weekends are official adjusted workdays, and individual factories have their own shifts. Confirm the exact appointment and attending staff; a general national calendar cannot establish their availability.</p></details><details><summary>Can I use a Chinese gender calendar to predict a baby’s sex?</summary><p>A traditional gender chart is folklore, not a reliable prediction method. It is separate from the date and holiday information covered here.</p></details></section>
<section id="sources"><h2>Calendar sources and scope</h2><p>Lunar dates and year names: Hong Kong Observatory <a href="https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/gts/time/calendar/pdf/files/2026e.pdf">2026</a> and <a href="https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/gts/time/calendar/pdf/files/2027e.pdf">2027 Gregorian–lunar conversion tables</a>. Mainland 2027 holiday periods: the State Council announcement reported by <a href="https://www.xinhuanet.com/20261105/d91d22dfe8aa4cd7a3bdbc0ad67ef68b/c.html">Xinhua on 5 November 2026</a>. Supplier scheduling questions are ChinaFactoryHost planning guidance, not official nationwide closure dates.</p><p>Verify supplier-specific dates before committing to flights, hotels or production-dependent visits. Calendar dates alone do not confirm appointments.</p></section>
</article></div>
</BaseLayout>
<script>
const count=document.getElementById('cny-countdown')!,caption=document.getElementById('countdown-caption')!;
function updateCountdown(){const parts=new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-CA',{timeZone:'Asia/Shanghai',year:'numeric',month:'2-digit',day:'2-digit'}).format(new Date());const difference=Math.round((Date.parse('2027-02-06T00:00:00Z')-Date.parse(parts+'T00:00:00Z'))/86400000);count.textContent=difference>0?String(difference):difference===0?'Today':'2027';caption.textContent=difference>0?'days to the Year of the Goat':difference===0?'Happy Chinese New Year':'The Year of the Goat began on 6 February';}updateCountdown();setInterval(updateCountdown,60000);
const boxes=[...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLInputElement>('.check-items input')];const storageKey='cfh-calendar-checklist-v1';try{const state=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(storageKey)||'[]');boxes.forEach((b,i)=>b.checked=state[i]===true)}catch{}
function refreshChecks(){const n=boxes.filter(b=>b.checked).length;document.getElementById('check-count')!.textContent=n+' of '+boxes.length+' confirmed';const progress=document.getElementById('check-progress') as HTMLProgressElement;progress.max=boxes.length;progress.value=n;try{localStorage.setItem(storageKey,JSON.stringify(boxes.map(b=>b.checked)))}catch{}}boxes.forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('change',refreshChecks));document.getElementById('reset-checklist')!.onclick=()=>{boxes.forEach(b=>b.checked=false);refreshChecks()};refreshChecks();
const links=[...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.calendar-nav a')];const sections=links.map(a=>document.querySelector(a.hash)).filter(Boolean) as HTMLElement[];let scheduled=false;function updateNav(){let active=sections[0];for(const section of sections){if(section.getBoundingClientRect().top<=160)active=section;}links.forEach(a=>{const on=a.hash==='#'+active.id;a.classList.toggle('is-active',on);if(on)a.setAttribute('aria-current','location');else a.removeAttribute('aria-current')});scheduled=false;}window.addEventListener('scroll',()=>{if(!scheduled){scheduled=true;requestAnimationFrame(updateNav)}},{passive:true});updateNav();
</script>
